Preallocated writes map and zero in runs, not per block

macOS preallocates a large file before copying into it, so every Finder
copy of any size writes into unwritten extents. Converting an unwritten
extent zeroes it first -- and both halves of that were done one block at
a time, because ext4_fs_append_inode_dblk asks the extent layer for
exactly one block and the zeroing loop then issued one command per block.

Measured on a real LUKS2 USB stick, a 522 MB Finder copy: 128,406 device
write commands for 996 MB, about 8 KB each at 1.2 ms apiece -- 6.3 MB/s,
roughly two and a half minutes. The three layers each reported their own
shape, which is what identified this: the kernel was sending 1 MB
requests and the shim was coalescing them into 255-block runs, so the
commands were being multiplied below both.

Reproduced offline exactly: 100 MB into a preallocated file cost 25,941
commands and wrote 210 MB, against 167 commands and 105 MB for the same
write into a fresh file.

Two changes, one cause:

* ext4_fs_append_inode_dblk_range asks for as many blocks as the caller
  can use and reports how many it got, so a write maps its whole run in
  one call. Indirect-mapped inodes (ext2/ext3) keep the single-block
  path; there is nothing to convert there and no range form to ask for.

* ext4_ext_zero_unwritten_range zeroes in runs of up to 1 MiB rather than
  a command per block, falling back to smaller pieces under memory
  pressure rather than failing the write.

Fresh files gain too: their blocks now come from one allocation instead
of one per block.

After: the same preallocated 100 MB costs 254 commands, contents
byte-identical, e2fsck clean. The bytes are still doubled -- the range is
zeroed and then overwritten -- and removing that needs the data write to
land before the extent is converted, which is a separate change and the
next one worth making.
--- a/src/ext4_extent.c
+++ b/src/ext4_extent.c
@@ -1978,9 +1978,28 @@
 					 uint32_t blocks_count)
 {
 	int err = EOK;
-	uint32_t i;
 	uint32_t block_size = ext4_sb_get_block_size(&inode_ref->fs->sb);
-	void *zeros = ext4_calloc(1, block_size);
+
+	/* The range is contiguous by construction, so it can be zeroed in runs
+	 * rather than a command per block. It matters because this is not a
+	 * rare path: macOS preallocates a large file before copying into it,
+	 * which is what an unwritten extent is, so every Finder copy of any
+	 * size lands here. Measured on a USB stick, 100 MB written into a
+	 * preallocated file cost 25,941 device commands where the same write
+	 * into a fresh file cost 167, and the copy ran at 6 MB/s. */
+	uint32_t chunk_blocks = (1u << 20) / block_size;
+	if (chunk_blocks == 0)
+		chunk_blocks = 1;
+	if (chunk_blocks > blocks_count)
+		chunk_blocks = blocks_count;
+
+	void *zeros = ext4_calloc(chunk_blocks, block_size);
+	while (!zeros && chunk_blocks > 1) {
+		/* Memory pressure is not a reason to fail a write; it is a
+		 * reason to do it in smaller pieces. */
+		chunk_blocks /= 2;
+		zeros = ext4_calloc(chunk_blocks, block_size);
+	}
 	if (!zeros)
 		return ENOMEM;
 
@@ -1993,11 +2012,16 @@
 	 * gives the caller a clean base its direct write can land on. It also
 	 * keeps data blocks out of the journal, where the transaction-dirty
 	 * path would have put them. */
